@namespace PgmStudio.Client.Components

@* One terrain-paint material, edited as a form (docs/world-export/terrain-painting.md §3). A material is
   recursive — a pattern's palette entry, a tint's neutral fallback, a stack's layer are themselves materials —
   so this component nests inside itself. It edits the theme JSON node in place, which IS the wire format the
   painter deserializes, so there is no second model of a material to drift from it. *@

<div class="material-editor @(Nested ? "material-editor--nested" : null)">
  @* What the entry is called heads its block rather than sharing the row with the controls: inline it was
     one more thing competing for the row's width, and it pushed the kind select out of line with every
     other kind select in the stack. *@
  @if (Label is not null) { <span class="material-editor-label">@Label</span> }

  @* One row of controls: what kind it is, how far it reaches, and the way to remove it. The extent belongs
     to the list that owns the entry rather than to the material, so it arrives as HeadExtra — and it is a
     CoordField, which carries its unit inside the box and so keeps the row one control tall. A labelled
     Field here stacked a label above its input, which left the number sitting lower than the select. *@
  <div class="material-editor-head">
    @* The kinds the *painter* accepts, in the order the schema publishes them — so a kind added server-side is
       offered here with a seed that works, rather than reaching no editor until someone adds it twice. *@
    <Select Options="@Select.Words(Schema.Kinds.Select(kind => kind.Kind), Schema.NameOf)"
            Value="@Kind" ValueChanged="ChangeKind" />
    @* Beside the kind, because it answers the same question: what is this material. A kind gives an empty
       recipe of that shape, a style gives one already written. It is an action rather than a state, so it
       falls back to its own placeholder — what is filled in is material JSON from that moment on, and the
       style it came from is not something the slot goes on holding. *@
    @if (Styles.Count > 0)
    {
      <Select Options="@StyleRows" Value="" Placeholder="Fill from a style…" @key="filled"
              Title="Replace this material with a copy of a saved style, then edit it here like any other"
              ValueChanged="FillFromStyle" />
    }
    @* What a kind is belongs beside the control that names it, and only where the material itself is being
       authored. A nested layer, band or stop is a reuse of a kind, not a second definition of it — a note
       on each one restated the same paragraph as many times as the stack was deep, which is what made a
       three-layer voronoi stack unreadable. *@
    @if (!Nested)
    {
      <HelpMark Open="helpOpen" Title="@($"What a {Schema.NameOf(Kind).ToLowerInvariant()} is")"
                OnClick="() => helpOpen = !helpOpen" />
    }
    @HeadExtra
    @if (OnRemove.HasDelegate)
    {
      <Button Variant="icon" Icon="trash-2" Title="Remove" OnClick="OnRemove" />
    }
  </div>

  @if (!Nested && helpOpen)
  {
    <div class="help-note-body">
      @* What the kind is, in the schema's own words — the same sentence the route answers an agent with, so a
         kind added server-side explains itself here without being written up twice. What follows it is this
         editor's own note about the controls, which the schema has no business carrying. *@
      @if (Schema.SummaryOf(Kind) is { Length: > 0 } summary)
      {
        <p>@summary</p>
      }
      @* Which facts about a cell it varies with, and therefore where it is legible at all: one reading the
         arc says nothing away from a perimeter, one reading inset draws rings and falls back off a footprint. *@
      @if (Schema.ReadsOf(Kind) is { Count: > 0 } reads)
      {
        <p class="section-desc">Varies with <strong>@string.Join(", ", reads)</strong>.</p>
      }
      @switch (Kind)
      {
        case MaterialKind.Solid:
          <p>The picker is the export's own palette, so the swatch is the colour that will land.</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.TeamTint:
          <p>Takes the owning team's colour on the same 0–15 scale as wool, so the block has to be one that
            colours by damage. Land with no team falls through to the neutral material.</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.Layered:
          <p>Bands along a distance, each claiming a stated thickness. <strong>Read along</strong> is which
            distance: <em>down the column</em> from the top of the bucket — grass over two dirt; <em>in from
            the edge</em>, so the bands run as rings round the shape — a cobble rim, two rings of stone brick,
            then a field; or <em>up from a world height</em>, which pins them to the world rather than to the
            column.</p>
          <p><strong>Past the last band</strong> is independent of the axis. A stack that owns its whole space
            repeats its last band, so a deeper bucket never falls through. A stack that is a band <em>inside</em>
            a larger space hands over instead, and what shows there is stone unless stated — which is what a
            rim two rings in and then the ground needs.</p>
          <p>The axes compose: a <em>down the column</em> stack whose first band is one course thick and is
            itself an <em>in from the edge</em> stack rings the top course and leaves the courses under it
            alone.</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.Voronoi:
          <p>Cells roughly one patch-size across, drawn as a <strong>ramp inward from the cell
            boundary</strong>. The first band sits on the boundary itself, so it draws the grid; each one
            after it is a ring further in, and the last takes the middle. A cell too small to reach a band
            never shows it, so small cells come out filled by whichever band they did reach. Deterministic —
            the same seed always paints it.</p>
          <p>@RiseNote</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.Cell:
          <p>Patches of one material each, any two of which may meet — the fabric a voronoi's diagram is not.
            <strong>Jitter</strong> is how far a patch's seed strays from its grid square (0% is the grid
            itself, 100% is shards); <strong>edge wander</strong> is how many blocks the boundaries wobble,
            which is what makes the patches organic rather than straight-edged.</p>
          <p>@RiseNote</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.Noise:
        case MaterialKind.Turbulence:
        case MaterialKind.Electric:
          <p>@FieldBlurb Cut into bands by the stops in order — the first takes the lowest slice of the
            field. Only neighbouring stops ever share a boundary, and the outermost two are accents rather
            than halves, so a stop list reads as a ramp from one material to the next. More octaves add finer
            detail without narrowing the field.</p>
          <p>@RiseNote</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.WallRun:
          <p>Stripes running along the wall that wrap the whole void-facing perimeter, corners included.
            Widths are in wall cells and the stripes repeat in order.</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.WallDiagonal:
          <p>The same stripes, sheared: each course starts <strong>slope</strong> cells further round the
            wall than the one below it, so they lean instead of standing. A slope of 1 moves one cell along
            per course up, which is 45°; larger lays them flatter and a negative slope leans them the other
            way. The shear is read off world height, so two walls of different heights standing together
            meet with their diagonals in line.</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.Checker:
          <p>Two materials alternating over squares of the given side. The board is laid <strong>in the face
            it paints</strong> — along the wall and up it on an outer wall, across the ground everywhere
            else — so it reads as squares on both rather than as stripes down a wall.</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.LogChecker:
          <p>One log alternating over squares of the given side: <strong>upright on one, on its side on the
            next</strong>. The grain runs vertically on one and across on the other, so a single block reads
            as a woven board — the timbering the hand-built houses on the corpus use. A log on its side is
            always laid <strong>along</strong> the wall, never across it, since a log laid across a wall
            points its sawn end at whoever is looking.</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.WallFrame:
          <p>Inks the wall's top and bottom courses and its corners, filling the panel between — a rectangle
            comes out outlined the way a comic panel is. A corner is decided by <strong>how far the outline
            turns</strong>, not by where it changes direction, so a staircased edge is not mistaken for one.
            <strong>Angle</strong> is how sharp a turn has to be to be inked: a right angle is 90°, a shallow
            bend far less, and a rounded shape never reaches a usable threshold — so a circle has no corners
            at all and the frame falls back to its top and bottom, which is a layer stack. The same number
            sets how far the ink wraps round each corner, since the turn ramps to a vertex rather than
            switching on at it.</p>
          break;
      }
    </div>
  }

  @* A stub is one row of a list the outline is already carrying: its kind, its extent and the way to remove
     it. What it is made of is edited by picking it in the outline, which is where its own nest lives. *@
  @if (!Stub)
  {
  <div class="material-editor-body">
    @switch (Kind)
    {
      case MaterialKind.Solid:
        <BlockPicker Blocks="Blocks" Id="@Int(ThemeFields.Id, 1)" Data="@Int(ThemeFields.Data, 0)" OnPick="PickSolid" />
        break;

      case MaterialKind.TeamTint:
        <BlockPicker Blocks="Blocks" Id="@Int(ThemeFields.BlockId, 159)" Data="0" ShowColors="false" OnPick="PickTintBlock" />
        <MaterialEditor Nested Flat="Flat" Label="@ChildLabel(ThemeFields.Neutral)" Blocks="Blocks" Styles="Styles" Node="Neutral" OnChanged="Changed" />
        break;

      case MaterialKind.Layered:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Read along">
            <Select Options="@Select.Words(BandAxes.All, BandAxes.Describe)" Value="@Axis" ValueChanged="SetAxis" />
          </Field>
          @if (Axis == BandAxes.Height)
          {
            <Field Label="From y">
              <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" step="1"
                     title="The world height the first band sits at."
                     value="@Int(ThemeFields.From, 0)" @onchange="SetFrom" />
            </Field>
          }
        </div>
        @foreach (var layer in List(ThemeFields.StackBands))
        {
          <MaterialEditor Nested Flat="Flat" Label="@EntryLabel(ThemeFields.StackBands, layer.Index)" Blocks="Blocks" Styles="Styles" @key="layer.Node"
                          Node="@layer.Material" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="@layer.Remove">
            <HeadExtra>
              <CoordField Prefix="@BandAxes.Extent(Axis)" Type="number" Value="@layer.Extent" OnChange="@layer.SetExtent"
                          min="1" step="1" title="@($"How many {BandAxes.Extent(Axis).ToLowerInvariant()} this band claims")" />
            </HeadExtra>
          </MaterialEditor>
        }
        @ListFoot(ThemeFields.StackBands, "Add band")
        <Field Label="Past the last band">
          <Chip Active="@(Ending == BandEndings.HandOver)" OnClick="ToggleEnding">@BandEndings.Describe(Ending)</Chip>
        </Field>
        @if (ShowsBeyond)
        {
          <Field Label="What shows there">
            <Chip Active="@JsonEdit.Has(Node, ThemeFields.Beyond)" OnClick="ToggleBeyond">
              @(JsonEdit.Has(Node, ThemeFields.Beyond) ? "A stated material" : "Stone")
            </Chip>
          </Field>
          @if (JsonEdit.Has(Node, ThemeFields.Beyond))
          {
            <MaterialEditor Nested Flat="Flat" Label="Beyond the stack" Blocks="Blocks" Styles="Styles"
                            Node="Beyond" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="ToggleBeyond" />
          }
        }
        break;

      case MaterialKind.Voronoi:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Patch size">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.CellSize, 10)" @onchange="SetCellSize" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Rise">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   title="Vertical period in blocks. 0 keeps the pattern flat."
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Rise, 0)" @onchange="SetRise" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Seed">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Seed, 0)" @onchange="SetSeed" />
          </Field>
        </div>
        @foreach (var band in List(ThemeFields.Bands))
        {
          <MaterialEditor Nested Flat="Flat" Label="@EntryLabel(ThemeFields.Bands, band.Index)" Blocks="Blocks" Styles="Styles" @key="band.Node"
                          Node="@band.Material" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="@band.Remove">
            <HeadExtra>
              <CoordField Prefix="Blocks" Type="number" Value="@band.Extent" OnChange="@band.SetExtent"
                          min="1" step="1" title="How many blocks in from the cell boundary this band runs" />
            </HeadExtra>
          </MaterialEditor>
        }
        @ListFoot(ThemeFields.Bands, "Add a band")
        break;

      case MaterialKind.Cell:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Patch size">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.CellSize, 10)" @onchange="SetCellSize" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Jitter %">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" max="100" step="5"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Jitter, 50)" @onchange="SetJitter" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Edge wander">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Warp, 4)" @onchange="SetWarp" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Rise">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   title="Vertical period in blocks. 0 keeps the pattern flat."
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Rise, 0)" @onchange="SetRise" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Seed">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Seed, 0)" @onchange="SetSeed" />
          </Field>
        </div>
        @foreach (var entry in List(ThemeFields.Palette))
        {
          <MaterialEditor Nested Flat="Flat" Label="@EntryLabel(ThemeFields.Palette, entry.Index)" Blocks="Blocks" Styles="Styles" @key="entry.Node"
                          Node="@entry.Material" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="@entry.Remove" />
        }
        @ListFoot(ThemeFields.Palette, "Add a patch material")
        break;

      case MaterialKind.Noise:
      case MaterialKind.Turbulence:
      case MaterialKind.Electric:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Scale">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Scale, 16)" @onchange="SetScale" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Octaves">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" max="8" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Octaves, 3)" @onchange="SetOctaves" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Rise">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   title="Vertical period in blocks. 0 keeps the pattern flat."
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Rise, 0)" @onchange="SetRise" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Seed">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Seed, 0)" @onchange="SetSeed" />
          </Field>
        </div>
        @foreach (var entry in List(ThemeFields.Stops))
        {
          <MaterialEditor Nested Flat="Flat" Label="@EntryLabel(ThemeFields.Stops, entry.Index)" Blocks="Blocks" Styles="Styles" @key="entry.Node"
                          Node="@entry.Material" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="@entry.Remove" />
        }
        @ListFoot(ThemeFields.Stops, "Add stop")
        break;

      case MaterialKind.WallRun:
        @foreach (var run in List(ThemeFields.Runs))
        {
          <MaterialEditor Nested Flat="Flat" Label="@EntryLabel(ThemeFields.Runs, run.Index)" Blocks="Blocks" Styles="Styles" @key="run.Node"
                          Node="@run.Material" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="@run.Remove">
            <HeadExtra>
              <CoordField Prefix="Cells" Type="number" Value="@run.Extent" OnChange="@run.SetExtent"
                          min="1" step="1" title="How many wall cells wide this stripe runs" />
            </HeadExtra>
          </MaterialEditor>
        }
        @ListFoot(ThemeFields.Runs, "Add stripe")
        break;

      case MaterialKind.WallDiagonal:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Slope">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" step="1"
                   title="Cells along the wall per course up. 1 is 45°, 0 is a vertical run."
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Slope, 1)" @onchange="SetSlope" />
          </Field>
        </div>
        @foreach (var run in List(ThemeFields.Runs))
        {
          <MaterialEditor Nested Flat="Flat" Label="@EntryLabel(ThemeFields.Runs, run.Index)" Blocks="Blocks" Styles="Styles" @key="run.Node"
                          Node="@run.Material" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="@run.Remove">
            <HeadExtra>
              <CoordField Prefix="Cells" Type="number" Value="@run.Extent" OnChange="@run.SetExtent"
                          min="1" step="1" title="How many wall cells wide this stripe runs" />
            </HeadExtra>
          </MaterialEditor>
        }
        @ListFoot(ThemeFields.Runs, "Add stripe")
        break;

      case MaterialKind.Checker:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Square">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" step="1"
                   title="The side of one square, in blocks"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Size, 1)" @onchange="SetSize" />
          </Field>
        </div>
        <MaterialEditor Nested Flat="Flat" Label="@ChildLabel(ThemeFields.Even)" Blocks="Blocks" Styles="Styles" Node="Even" OnChanged="Changed" />
        <MaterialEditor Nested Flat="Flat" Label="@ChildLabel(ThemeFields.Odd)" Blocks="Blocks" Styles="Styles" Node="Odd" OnChanged="Changed" />
        break;

      case MaterialKind.LogChecker:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Square">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" step="1"
                   title="The side of one square, in blocks"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Size, 1)" @onchange="SetSize" />
          </Field>
        </div>
        @* The block is picked whole and only its species is kept: the two bits above it are the axis, and
           the axis is what this pattern is turning. *@
        <BlockPicker Blocks="Blocks" Id="@Int(ThemeFields.Id, 162)" Data="@Int(ThemeFields.Data, 0)" OnPick="PickSolid" />
        break;

      case MaterialKind.WallFrame:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Corner angle">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" max="180" step="1"
                   title="How far the outline must turn to count as a corner. 90° is a right angle; a rounded shape never reaches 45."
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Angle, 45)" @onchange="SetAngle" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Courses">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" step="1"
                   title="Courses inked along the top and the bottom"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Thickness, 1)" @onchange="SetThickness" />
          </Field>
        </div>
        <MaterialEditor Nested Flat="Flat" Label="@ChildLabel(ThemeFields.Edge)" Blocks="Blocks" Styles="Styles" Node="Edge" OnChanged="Changed" />
        <MaterialEditor Nested Flat="Flat" Label="@ChildLabel(ThemeFields.Fill)" Blocks="Blocks" Styles="Styles" Node="Panel" OnChanged="Changed" />
        break;
    }
  </div>
  }
</div>

@code {
    @* The footer under one of a material's lists: add a single entry, or fill the list from a whole family.
       One template rather than five copies — every list carries the same pair, and the field it acts on is the
       only thing that differs between them. The select shows the family the list currently holds, so it reads
       as a label until an author narrows the list and as an offer again once they have. *@
    private RenderFragment ListFoot(string field, string addLabel) => @<div class="material-editor-foot">
        <Button Icon="plus" OnClick="() => Add(field)">@addLabel</Button>
        @if (Families.Any())
        {
            <Select Options="@FamilyRows" Value="@MatchedFamily(field)" Placeholder="Fill from a family…"
                    Title="Fill this list with a whole family of blocks, then remove the ones this ground does not use"
                    ValueChanged="family => ApplyFamily(field, family)" />
        }
    </div>;
}
